From: Sahan Nissanka <sanoptional@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: simple: awb: Remove the hardcoded 4.0 colour gain clamp

The grey-world AWB clamps both colour gains at 4.0. Any sensor whose red
response is weaker than a quarter of green can therefore never be white
balanced, and sits permanently short of neutral with a cyan cast.

On an OV5675 (Dell Latitude 7320 Detachable, IPU6, software ISP), pointed at
lit white paper, raw Bayer read off the sensor with a dark frame subtracted
gives a native balance of R/G 0.164, B/G 0.665 - so neutral needs a red gain
of 6.1. The algorithm instead reports:

  IPASoftAwb awb.cpp:103 gain R/B: Vector { 4, 1, 1.54655 }; temperature: 6448

Red is pinned at exactly 4.0 on every frame while blue computes normally at
1.5465 (against 1.50 predicted), confirming the maths and the black level are
correct and only the ceiling is at fault. Measured output is R/G 0.372
against a target of 1.000; webcamtests.com independently reports hue 178
degrees, i.e. cyan, at 34% saturation on a white wall.

Drop the ceiling and keep only the division-by-zero guard, matching what
AwbGrey in libipa does.

Signed-off-by: Sahan Nissanka <sanoptional@gmail.com>

--- a/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/awb.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/awb.cpp
@@ -95,15 +95,16 @@
 	const RGB<uint64_t> sum = stats->sum_.max(offset + minValid) - offset;
 
 	/*
-	 * Calculate red and blue gains for AWB.
-	 * Clamp max gain at 4.0, this also avoids 0 division.
+	 * Calculate red and blue gains for AWB. Guard against division by
+	 * zero, but do not impose a maximum: a hardcoded ceiling leaves
+	 * sensors with a weak red response permanently short of neutral.
 	 */
 	auto &gains = context.activeState.awb.gains;
 	gains = { {
-		sum.r() <= sum.g() / 4 ? 4.0f : static_cast<float>(sum.g()) / sum.r(),
+		static_cast<float>(sum.g()) / std::max<uint64_t>(sum.r(), 1),
 		1.0,
-		sum.b() <= sum.g() / 4 ? 4.0f : static_cast<float>(sum.g()) / sum.b(),
+		static_cast<float>(sum.g()) / std::max<uint64_t>(sum.b(), 1),
 	} };
 
